Experiment: Buffer Overflow Demonstration using GDB (Kali Linux)
🎯 Aim

To demonstrate a buffer overflow vulnerability and analyze it using the GDB debugger in Kali Linux.

🎯 Objectives
To understand buffer overflow vulnerability
To study stack memory behavior
To analyze program execution using GDB
To observe program crash due to overflow
🧠 Concept (Short for Viva)

A buffer overflow occurs when input exceeds the allocated buffer size and overwrites adjacent memory.

Stack contains:
Local variables
Return address

👉 Overflow can overwrite return address → causes crash or abnormal behavior

⚙️ Tools Used
6
GCC (compiler)
GDB (debugger)
🧾 Requirements
Kali Linux
GCC and GDB installed
🪜 Procedure (Step-by-Step)
🔹 Step 1: Create Vulnerable Program
nano vuln.c

Paste:

#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    char buffer[10];
    printf("Enter input: ");
    scanf("%s", buffer);
    printf("You entered: %s\n", buffer);
    return 0;
}

Save file.

🔹 Step 2: Compile Program
gcc -g -fno-stack-protector -z execstack vuln.c -o vuln
🔹 Step 3: Run Program
./vuln

Enter large input:

AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA

👉 Program crashes (segmentation fault)

🔹 Step 4: Debug using GDB
gdb ./vuln
🔹 Step 5: Run inside GDB
run

Enter long input again.

🔹 Step 6: Analyze Registers
info registers
🔹 Step 7: View Stack Memory
x/20x $esp
👀 Observations
Input exceeded buffer size
Stack memory was overwritten
Program crashed due to segmentation fault
Registers showed corrupted values
Demonstrated unsafe input handling
✅ Result

The buffer overflow vulnerability was successfully demonstrated using GDB. The experiment showed how improper input handling can lead to memory corruption and program crashes.
